1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,750
Pre-sales is the most expensive work nobody bills. A statement of work, a
delivery plan, a compliance review, written by your most senior people, before
a contract exists.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,750
This turns a client brief into all three, in minutes. And it shows its
evidence.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,750
It starts with the brief, spoken. Watch the words arrive in the box while they
are being said.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:33,750
A live model drafts the contract. Claude. A second, GPT, audits it against the
compliance corpus. Nothing is published on one model's opinion.

5
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:42,750
The audit passes. A second person opens the run by its identifier and approves
that exact version.

6
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:50,750
Twenty-six objects in Jira, one page in Notion, reported separately, with a
link to each.

7
00:00:51,000 --> 00:01:00,750
Jira fills itself: six epics, nineteen stories, every one with an epic and a
due date. The plan states its assumptions rather than hiding them.

8
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,750
On the timeline the epics line up against the schedule the reviewer approved.

9
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:13,750
Notion carries the summary and the compliance position for the client.

10
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:23,750
And the contract itself. Clauses, numbering, evidence, governing law. The
artefact you sign.

11
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,750
Plus the PDF, the CSV and the audit report, from the same run.

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:36,750
If a destination refuses, the run keeps what succeeded. The retry sends only
what failed.

13
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:46,750
Three days become three minutes, and every clause traces to a source. The demo
and this video are on GitHub, under Sergey Ger SOWSprint dot A I.
